Evaluation of Disclosure Controls and Procedures

The successor custodian, who performs the functions of principal executive officer and principal financial officer, evaluated the effectiveness of the Company’s disclosure controls and procedures (as defined in Rules 13a-15(e) and 15d-15(e) under the Exchange Act) as of June 30, 2026. He was appointed on October 6, 2026 and his evaluation is based on the records available to him and on the findings in the Order. Based on that evaluation, he concluded that the Company’s disclosure controls and procedures were not effective as of June 30, 2026 to provide reasonable assurance that information required to be disclosed by the Company in the reports filed or submitted by it under the Exchange Act is (i) recorded, processed, summarized and reported within the time periods specified in the SEC’s rules and forms and (ii) accumulated and communicated to management, including the principal executive officer, as appropriate to allow timely decisions regarding required disclosure, due to the material weaknesses described below.

Based on that evaluation, the Company had “material weaknesses” (as such term is defined below) in its control environment and financial reporting process consisting of the following as of June 30, 2026:

1) lack of a functioning board of directors and audit committee resulting in ineffective oversight in the establishment and monitoring of required internal control and procedures; one individual acted as custodian, sole director and sole officer and authorized, executed and recorded transactions without independent oversight;

2) absence of controls to identify, approve and disclose related-party transactions; the Company had no director other than the former custodian, so transactions with entities controlled by him were not reviewed or approved by any disinterested person, and no independent valuations were obtained;

3) absence of effective controls over the issuance of equity securities, including compliance with the Articles of Incorporation, the authorized share capital and the Court’s stay of share issuances;
